          <h3>Authentic Cultural Experiences (Not Tourist Performances)</h3>
          
          <h4>1. Swahili Home Cooking Class</h4>
          <ul>
            <li>Join a local family in Stone Town or Jambiani</li>
            <li>Shop Darajani Market for ingredients with your host</li>
            <li>Learn pilau, biryani, urojo (Zanzibar mix), coconut rice</li>
            <li>Share the meal — <strong>Dhow Coast tip:</strong> Request vegetarian/halal options</li>
          </ul>

          <h4>2. Seaweed Farming with Jambiani Women</h4>
          <ul>
            <li>Work alongside women who've farmed seaweed for generations</li>
            <li>Learn planting, harvesting, drying process at low tide</li>
            <li>Understand economic impact on village women's cooperatives</li>
            <li><strong>Best time:</strong> Morning low tide (check tide tables)</li>
          </ul>

          <h4>3. Dhow Building in Nungwi</h4>
          <ul>
            <li>Visit traditional dhow yard where craftsmen build by hand</li>
            <li>No power tools — hand-sawn planks, coconut fiber caulking</li>
            <li>Talk to master builders about Swahili maritime heritage</li>
            <li><strong>Dhow Coast tip:</strong> Combine with Mnemba snorkeling afternoon</li>
          </ul>

          <h4>4. Kuza Cave Cultural Visit (Jambiani)</h4>
          <ul>
            <li>Sacred freshwater cave used for centuries by locals</li>
            <li>Learn Swahili rituals, traditional medicine, oral histories</li>
            <li>Community-run — fees support village projects</li>
            <li>Swim in crystal-clear freshwater pools</li>
          </ul>

          <h4>5. Spice Farm with Cultural Context (Kidwaju/Kizimkazi)</h4>
          <ul>
            <li>Beyond sensory walk — learn spice trade's impact on Swahili culture</li>
            <li>Arab, Indian, African influences on language, cuisine, architecture</li>
            <li>Taste fresh cloves, vanilla, cinnamon, nutmeg, black pepper</li>
            <li>Take home farm-grown spices at local prices</li>
          </ul>

          <h4>6. Stone Town Evening Culture Walk</h4>
          <ul>
            <li>Taarab music at Old Fort or Cultural Arts Centre</li>
            <li>Forodhani night market — eat where locals eat</li>
            <li>Visit Freddie Mercury House (Zanzibar's most famous son)</li>
            <li>Rooftop chai at Emerson on Hurumzi with sunset views</li>
          </ul>

          <h3>Cultural Etiquette Quick Guide</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Dress modestly in villages (shoulders/knees covered)</li>
            <li>Ask before photographing people — most welcome it if asked</li>
            <li>Use right hand for eating/giving — left is traditionally unclean</li>
            <li>Learn basic Swahili: "Asante" (thanks), "Samahani" (excuse me), "Karibu" (welcome)</li>
            <li>Ramadan awareness: no eating/drinking in public during daylight hours</li>
          </ul>

          <h3>Why These Are Different From Standard Tours</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Community-owned:</strong> Fees go directly to families/cooperatives</li>
            <li><strong>No staged performances:</strong> You participate in real daily activities</li>
            <li><strong>Small groups:</strong> Max 6 people for intimate experience</li>
            <li><strong>Local guides:</strong> Born in the villages, not imported from Stone Town</li>
            <li><strong>Flexible timing:</strong> We adjust to tide, weather, family schedules</li>
          </ul>
